Optimal Solution - Use pairwise comparison to check if any word is a substring of another using built-in string matching.
                   “For each word, I check if it exists as a substring in any other word. Since constraints are small, a brute-force O(n²) approach with contains() is sufficient.”

class Solution {
    public List<String> stringMatching(String[] words) {
        List<String> ans = new ArrayList<>();  // Stores words that are substrings of another word
        // Iterate over each word as potential substring
        for (int i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
            // Compare with every other word
            for (int j = 0; j < words.length; j++) {
                // Skip comparing the word with itself
                if (i == j)
                    continue;
                // If words[i] is a substring of words[j]
                if (words[j].contains(words[i])) {
                    // Add it to result
                    ans.add(words[i]);
                    // No need to check further once found
                    break;
                }
            }
        }        
        // Return all substring words
        return ans;
    }
}

Time Complexity :- O(n^2 * m).
Space Complexity :- O(1).
